---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const words = [
  { ig: 'Ifo chakpii!', note: 'How a folktale is started at home. The storyteller calls it out and everyone listening answers “Wọo!”' },
  { ig: 'Nne-nne', note: 'Grandmother. Chidi’s Nne-nne is the one telling the story.' },
  { ig: 'Chi', note: 'Your personal spirit, the part of the divine that goes with you through life. In Igbo belief, your chi has a say in what you can and can’t do.' },
  { ig: 'Ụmụagụ', note: 'Ojadili’s village in the book.' },
  { ig: 'Echi dị ime', note: 'Tomorrow is pregnant. Nobody knows what tomorrow will bring.' },
  { ig: 'Onye ji onye n’ala ji onwe ya', note: 'Whoever holds another person down holds himself down too.' },
  { ig: 'Ala Mmụọ', note: 'The land of the spirits.' },
];
---
<Base title="The story behind it" description="The Igbo folktale of Ojadili, the wrestler who fought his chi, and why Dubem Gbuzue retold it for Igbo kids growing up away from home.">
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>The story behind it</h1>

      <div class="cols">
        <div class="prose">
          <h2>The old tale</h2>
          <p>
            Ojadili is a story Igbo families have told for a long time. He’s a wrestler so strong
            that no one in the world of people can throw him, so he goes into the world of spirits
            and keeps on winning there. In the end he faces one more opponent, his own <i lang="ig">chi</i>,
            and it’s the one fight he can’t win.
          </p>
          <p>
            The way I heard it, the story is about pride, and about the limits of strength when you
            stop listening to anyone. It also carries a very Igbo idea: you and your chi work
            together, and nobody goes far by fighting the thing that walks with them.
          </p>

          <h2>Why I made this book</h2>
          <p>
            I grew up in Nigeria, in Nnokwa, and came to Sydney to study. I stayed and built a family
            here. My kids are growing up a long way from where I grew up, and I’ve found it hard to
            give them the stories, the words and the feel of home that I had without trying.
          </p>
          <p>
            So I made the book I wanted to read with them. It’s rooted in Igbo culture, in the way
            people talk, the proverbs and the words, but it’s kept simple enough that a kid who
            doesn’t speak Igbo can follow every page.
          </p>
          <p>
            Book One is dedicated to my son Zimobi, and to all kids who have left their place of
            heritage and want to get back to it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
          <h2>What I changed</h2>
          <p>
            I added Chidi and his grandmother. Chidi is a twelve-year-old in Sydney who plays football,
            and the whole tale reaches him the way it reached me, from someone older, starting with
            <i lang="ig">Ifo chakpii!</i> A lot of kids reading this book live where Chidi lives.
          </p>
          <p>
            I gave Ojadili a home, the village of Ụmụagụ, and a reason to fight. In the book his father
            is a palm-wine tapper who falls from a palm tree. The boy makes a promise to his chi, and
            part of that promise is the proverb that names Chapter 2:
            <i lang="ig">onye ji onye n’ala ji onwe ya</i>.
          </p>
          <p>
            The fight with his chi stays where it belongs, at the very end. What happens to Ojadili
            after he loses is for the next book.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section glossary-wrap">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Igbo words in the book</h2>
      <dl class="glossary">
        {words.map((w) => (
          <div>
            <dt lang="ig">{w.ig}</dt>
            <dd>{w.note}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .cols {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 5rem);
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }
  .cols h2 { font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 3.4vw, 2.6rem); margin-top: 0.5em; }
  .glossary-wrap { background: var(--night); color: var(--nzu); padding-top: clamp(3.5rem, 8vw, 6rem) !important; }
  .glossary {
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(min(100%, 19rem), 1fr));
    gap: 2rem 3rem;
  }
  .glossary dt {
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    line-height: 1.05;
    color: var(--palm);
    margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
  }
  .glossary dd { margin: 0; max-width: 30rem; }
  @media (max-width: 760px) { .cols { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
</style>
